WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.717
The WAL is the write-ahead log.

00:00:04.717 --> 00:00:07.434
It records every change as an old value and a new value.

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:10.150
Here is how it works.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.867
The next page is how recovery uses it.

00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:16.076
Every update is three writes, in order.

00:00:16.076 --> 00:00:19.284
Say value A is updated from nineteen to twenty-two point eight.

00:00:19.284 --> 00:00:22.493
First, it appends the change to the log, the old value and the new.

00:00:22.493 --> 00:00:25.701
Tiny, a few tens of bytes, durable at once.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:28.910
Then it updates A's page in the RAM buffer, in place.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.118
Fast, but volatile.

00:00:32.118 --> 00:00:35.327
Both happen now.

00:00:35.327 --> 00:00:38.535
Later, the whole sixty-four megabyte page flushes to disk.

00:00:38.535 --> 00:00:41.744
One write, start to end.

00:00:41.744 --> 00:00:44.952
The log is durable before the page ever reaches disk.

00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:48.161
That is write-ahead.

00:00:48.161 --> 00:00:51.482
Let's dig into the WAL rows.

00:00:51.482 --> 00:00:54.802
Each row is small.

00:00:54.802 --> 00:00:58.123
A header, the transaction, the location, and the old and new value.

00:00:58.123 --> 00:01:01.443
About forty bytes.

00:01:01.443 --> 00:01:04.763
Here, value A is updated nineteen to twenty-two point eight, then B, twenty-three to twenty-seven point six.

00:01:04.763 --> 00:01:08.084
Two rows.

00:01:08.084 --> 00:01:11.404
Then a commit record marks where the transaction ends.

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:14.725
These are the rows the next page runs undo and redo against.

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.744
Change Tracking asked for any past version.

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:20.762
The log is the answer.

00:01:20.762 --> 00:01:23.781
Here is the log over time, every row an old value and a new value.

00:01:23.781 --> 00:01:26.799
Pick any past moment.

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:29.818
Replay the rows up to there, and you have the exact state then.

00:01:29.818 --> 00:01:32.836
A twenty-two point eight, B twenty-seven point six.

00:01:32.836 --> 00:01:35.855
Forward, redo the new values.

00:01:35.855 --> 00:01:38.873
Back, undo the old ones.

00:01:38.873 --> 00:01:41.892
The log is the version history.

00:01:41.892 --> 00:01:45.668
A WAL row is cheap for two reasons.

00:01:45.668 --> 00:01:49.443
It is tiny, and it only appends.

00:01:49.443 --> 00:01:53.219
Compare them.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:56.994
Without the log, each change is a random write to its own sixty-four megabyte page.

00:01:56.994 --> 00:02:00.770
About thirteen milliseconds.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.546
With the log, every change is a forty byte row, appended right after the last.

00:02:04.546 --> 00:02:08.321
The WAL flushes in small sixty-four kilobyte pages, about thirteen microseconds each.

00:02:08.321 --> 00:02:12.097
A thousand times less.

00:02:12.097 --> 00:02:15.872
And with battery-backed non-volatile RAM, a row is durable at RAM speed, about a hundred nanoseconds.

00:02:15.872 --> 00:02:19.648
A hundred thousand times less than the page flush.

00:02:19.648 --> 00:02:22.894
Last question.

00:02:22.894 --> 00:02:26.140
Why must the log go first?

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.386
The update and the flush happen at different times, so a crash can land between them.

00:02:29.386 --> 00:02:32.632
Suppose the data page lands first, and the server dies before the log row.

00:02:32.632 --> 00:02:35.879
The data changed, with no record of why.

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:39.125
Unrecoverable.

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:42.371
Now flip it, the way write-ahead demands.

00:02:42.371 --> 00:02:45.617
The log is on disk first.

00:02:45.617 --> 00:02:48.863
A crash before the data page moves still leaves the change in the log, ready to re-apply.

00:02:48.863 --> 00:02:52.109
Surviving a real crash is the next two pages.

00:02:52.109 --> 00:02:55.019
So one log gives you three things.

00:02:55.019 --> 00:02:57.929
Any past version, from the old and new in every row.

00:02:57.929 --> 00:03:00.840
Cheap writes, small and sequential.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.750
And a safe order, the log before the data.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.660
Its fourth payoff is surviving a crash.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.570
That is undo and redo, next.
